<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Megali Idea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1844">1844</a>–<a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Great Idea</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Megali Idea, or Great Idea, was the aim of Greek nationalism to unite all Greek-inhabited lands in one state, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> as its capital. Named in a speech by Ioannis Kolettis in 1844, it guided Greek policy for nearly eighty years and led to the gain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, Macedonia and other lands. It ended with the defeat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> in 1922 and the exchange of populations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/megali-idea.jpg" alt="Megali Idea" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Signed by Gr. Kouriotis (Γρ. Κουριώτης) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Great_Greece_(Megali_Hellas)_Venizelos_c1920.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The expression first appeared in the debates between Prime Minister Ioannis Kolettis and King Otto that preceded the constitution of 1844, but the hope behind it was older. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> had long looked for liberation from Ottoman rule and a restoration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>; folklore told that the last emperor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-xi-palaiologos">Constantine XI Palaiologos</a>, had been turned to marble and lay beneath the Golden Gate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> awaiting the call to reconquer the city. The state created in 1830 was designed by the Great Powers, and most ethnic Greeks still lived outside it, within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p><p>The new state was imagined as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> of Two Continents and Five Seas, reaching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-sea">Ionian Sea</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-region">Macedonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. When the Danish prince Wilhelm Georg was elected king in 1863 he was styled King of the Hellenes rather than King of Greece, implying a king of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> wherever they lived.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The Ionian Islands were acquired in 1864. In 1897, with violence growing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, a Greek force of 1,500 men under Colonel Timoleon Vassos landed at Kolymbari on 1 February and proclaimed the island's union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Irregulars then raided Turkish outposts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> without orders, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> declared war. The Greek army fell back, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> was signed in May, and Greece lost small border territories and paid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a>; Crete became an autonomous state with Prince George of Greece as High Commissioner.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleftherios-venizelos">Eleftherios Venizelos</a>, a Cretan who became prime minister after the Goudi coup of 1909, was a major proponent of the idea, and under him Greek territory doubled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a> of 1912–13. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> in 1917 and afterwards gained a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over Smyrna and its hinterland, where a referendum was to follow five years of Greek administration, and a frontier in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> a few miles from the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Turkish revolutionaries defeated the Greek army and expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lausanne">Treaty of Lausanne</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-thrace">Eastern Thrace</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imbros">Imbros</a>, Tenedos and Smyrna. During the conflict 151,892 Greeks had already fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>; the treaty moved 1,104,216 Greeks from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, while 380,000 Turks left Greece. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> arranged a loan of 150 million francs to help settle the refugees. In 1930 Venizelos paid an official visit to Turkey, where he proposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dodecanese">Dodecanese</a> were acquired after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, in 1947.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Megali Idea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Military affairs were bound up with the ‘<b>Great Idea</b>’ (Megali Idea), the aim of uniting all Greeks in one state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Military</a></li></ul></section>
